  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "to age; to grow old; to look older",
      "explanation": "To show signs of aging in one's appearance. Often used to describe someone who looks older than their actual age or who has visibly aged."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "16742_fukeru_ex1",
      "japanese": "ずいぶん{老|ふ}けたね。",
      "english": "You've aged quite a bit, haven't you?",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "16742_fukeru_ex2",
      "japanese": "{苦労|くろう}が{多|おお}くて{老|ふ}けて{見|み}える。",
      "english": "The many hardships make them look old.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "16742_fukeru_ex3",
      "japanese": "{十|じゅう}{年|ねん}ぶりに{会|あ}った{友人|ゆうじん}はすっかり{老|ふ}けていて{驚|おどろ}いた。",
      "english": "I was surprised to see how much my friend had aged when I met them after ten years.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "USAGE:\nFocuses on visible changes in appearance due to aging. Can be blunt or even rude when said directly to someone. The past form {老|ふ}けた is often used as a modifier meaning \"aged-looking.\"\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {老|ふ}けて{見|み}える (to look old)\n- すっかり{老|ふ}ける (to age completely)\n- {急|きゅう}に{老|ふ}ける (to age suddenly)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {年|とし}を{取|と}る: to grow older — neutral and objective\n- {老|お}いる: to grow old — more literary, focuses on the process of aging itself",
